Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/30/2026

FADE IN:

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Period live-action. A gaunt adult in a dark waistcoat sits rigidly at a table beside a closed tin lantern. His eyes are bright, his hands unnaturally still.

THE NARRATOR

True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

THE NARRATOR

The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute. I heard all things in the heaven and in the earth. I heard many things in hell.

THE NARRATOR

Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

An elderly man with silver hair sleeps beneath a heavy bedspread. One pale blue eye, clouded by a film, is shut.

A narrow door opens by a fraction. THE NARRATOR slips in a dark lantern, closed tight against the light, then slowly inserts his head.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult. For his gold I had no desire.

The old man turns softly in his sleep. The narrator freezes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I think it was his eye! yes, it was this! He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

A single thin ray escapes the lantern and finds only the old man's closed eyelid.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

And this I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Sunlight reaches the old man's orderly room. THE NARRATOR enters openly, cheerful and composed.

THE NARRATOR

Good morning, old man. How have you passed the night?

THE OLD MAN

Very well. Very well.

The narrator smiles warmly. Behind the smile, his gaze fixes on the old man's clouded eye.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The narrator turns the closed lantern in both hands. His calm has the strain of a held breath.

THE NARRATOR

You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me.

THE NARRATOR

You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

THE NARRATOR

Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The shutters are fastened against robbers. The room is black as pitch.

THE NARRATOR eases the latch. The door moves more slowly than a watch's minute hand.

He suppresses a chuckle.

THE OLD MAN jolts upright in bed.

THE OLD MAN

Who's there?

The narrator stands motionless behind the door. Silence fills the room.

An hour seems to pass. The old man remains upright, listening into the darkness.

THE OLD MAN

It is nothing but the wind in the chimney.

A faint creak in the wall.

THE OLD MAN

It is only a mouse crossing the floor.

The old man grips the bedspread. A low, stifled groan rises from him.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

The narrator opens the lantern a very little. A dim ray, thin as a spider's thread, crosses the room.

It falls full upon the old man's open eye.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

The old man's eye shines dull blue beneath its hideous veil. Nothing else is caught in the ray.

A low, quick beating begins beneath the silence.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

The narrator's lantern hand trembles, then locks still.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The beating quickens.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The narrator leans toward the lantern, listening. The quick, muffled beat seems to come from the walls around him.

THE NARRATOR

It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage.

THE NARRATOR

I scarcely breathed. I held the lantern motionless. I tried how steadily I could maintain the ray upon the eye.

THE NARRATOR

But the beating grew louder, louder! I thought the heart must burst.

THE NARRATOR

A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The old man's heart pounds louder. THE NARRATOR's face tightens in the doorway.

THE NARRATOR throws open the lantern and leaps into the room.

THE OLD MAN shrieks once.

The narrator drags him from the bed to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

The heart beats on, muffled beneath the bed.

THE NARRATOR watches, smiling gaily.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

The old man's hand lies still beside the bed frame.

The beating weakens.

THE NARRATOR places his hand against the old man's chest and waits.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

THE NARRATOR lifts the bed aside and examines the body.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I placed my hand upon the heart and held it there many minutes. There was no pulsation. He was stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR works hastily and silently. He dismembers the corpse, then lifts three floor planks.

A tub beneath his work has caught every drop.

He places the remains between the scantlings and replaces the boards with care. The floor is seamless.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

THE NARRATOR runs his palm across the replaced boards.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever. I had been too wary for that.

A clock bell sounds four.

INT. FRONT HALL - PRE-DAWN

A hard knocking comes from the street door.

THE NARRATOR descends the narrow stairs with a light heart and opens the door to three neatly dressed police officers.

THE NARRATOR

The shriek was my own in a dream.

The officers listen politely.

THE NARRATOR

The old man is absent in the country.

The narrator leads them upstairs, confident and welcoming.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The officers inspect the undisturbed treasures. THE NARRATOR opens drawers, gestures to every corner, and bids them search well.

Satisfied, the officers sit in chairs the narrator has brought into the room.

THE NARRATOR places his own chair directly over the hidden body and sits.

THE NARRATOR

Rest from your fatigues.

The officers chat pleasantly among themselves. The narrator answers cheerily.

A faint ringing comes into the narrator's ears.

He touches his temple.

The ringing gathers shape: a low, dull, quick sound.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I fancied a ringing in my ears: but still they sat and still chatted. The ringing became more distinct.

The narrator talks faster, forcing a smile.

THE NARRATOR

His treasures are secure—undisturbed.

The officers continue their pleasant conversation. The low beat grows louder beneath the floor.

THE NARRATOR rises, suddenly pale.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)

I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The narrator paces with heavy strides. The officers watch, politely puzzled.

The beating gains force, quick and relentless.

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The narrator sits again beside the lantern. The pounding now seems to shake the table itself.

THE NARRATOR

No doubt I now grew very pale;—but I talked more fluently, and with a heightened voice.

THE NARRATOR

Why would they not be gone?

THE NARRATOR

They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The narrator's fist strikes the table. The lantern rattles.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

THE NARRATOR swings his chair from the floor and grates it violently across the boards.

The beat rises over the scrape, over the officers' pleasant voices, over everything.

He staggers over the concealed planks, foaming with terror.

THE NARRATOR

Villains!

The officers fall silent.

THE NARRATOR

Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!

He points down with both hands.

THE NARRATOR

It is the beating of his hideous heart!

INT. NARRATOR'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

The lantern is closed. The narrator sits perfectly still, listening into darkness.

THE NARRATOR

How, then, am I mad?

FADE OUT.
